Types of VCS :
--------------
There are 2 types of VCS:
i)Centralized VCS         ii) De-Centralized VCS

1)Centralized VCS: The name itself indicates that , this type contains only one central repository and every developer should be connected to that repository.
------------------    -the total project code should be stored in the central repository.
                      -If 4 developers are there , still we have only one repository.
-This type of VCS is very easy to setup and use.
EX : CVS , SVN , Perforce , TFS , ClearCase etc............

-Problems with Centralized VCS :
--------------------------------
1)Since everything is stored in a single place ,which causes single point of failure . If something goes wrong to central repository then recovery is very difficult.
2)All commit/checkout operations done by connecting to the central repository via n/w. if n/w outage ,then no version control to the developer.
3)since no local operations and every version control operation should be remote operation hence operations will become slower, which causes performance issues.
4)Organization of Central repository is very complex if number of developers and files increases.


2)Distributed / De - Centralized VCS:
-------------------------------------
-The name itself indicates the repository is distributed and every developers workspace contains a local copy of the repository . there is no question of central repository.
-If 4 developers are there then 4 repositories will be there 
1)The checkout and commit operations will be performed locally, hence performance is more.
2)To perform checkout and commit operations n/w is not required ,hence if there is any n/w outage  still version control is applicable.
3)If something goes to wrong to any repository there is a chance to recover.there is no question of single point of failure.
4)To perform push and pull operations n/w must be required , but these operations are not most common operations and we will perform these very rarely.

Note : 
      1) Commit and checkout operations will be performed between workspace and repository.
     workspace --->checkout--->repository
     repository--->checkout--->workspace.
      2) push and pull operations will be performed between repositories
                one repository---push--->another repository
                another repository--->pull--->other repository

Distributed VCS with Remote Repository:
----------------------------------------
-Remote repository is not central repository :
--------------------------------------------
1)Every developer has his own local copy of repository . It is not centralized and it is distributed only.
2)commit and checkout operations won't be performed on remote repository and these will be performed on local repository only.
-The main objective of remote repo is share our work to peer developers.
-High availability , speed and there is no single point of failure are main reasons for popularity of this model.

Ex:Git,Mercurial,Fossil








